window.GAKS_SEARCH = [{"t":"The learning path","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path.html","s":"draft","x":"The learning path Definition The ordered index of this corpus's concept documents, junior-first. The corpus is flat and wiki-linked; this page is the sequence a reader new to test automation can follow, each entry naming its prerequisites. Why it exists ADR-taf-v1-002-01 makes the delivered project a learning surface: every concept the generated code embodies is a page here, and generated files cite their concepts with @concept lines the checker enforces. This page answers \"where do I start?\" The path 1. What a test is → CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-arrange-act-assert 2. Finding things on a page → CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-locator-ladder 3. Knowing where you are → the state marker (SPEC-taf-v1-001-01, states) 4. The model → CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-sut-model 5. From model to suite → the emitter and the manifest (SPEC-taf-v1-001-01) 6. pytest mechanics — fixtures, yield, markers, parametrize (as the Python emitter lands) 7. Playwright mechanics — auto-waiting, contexts, isolation (same) 8. Java parity — what \"equivalent suite\" means (lane D-4) 9. The graph — walks, seeds, coverage (lane F-6) Entries 6–9 are placeholders: the concept pages arrive with the lanes that embody them, because the checker refuses a citation with no page and a page nothing cites."},{"t":"The SUT model","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-sut-model","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-sut-model.html","s":"draft","x":"The SUT model Definition One JSON document that declares a system under test: its states (places a journey can be, each with a marker that proves arrival), its elements (named locators), its actions (steps the SUT offers — the edges of the graph), its journeys (named paths through the actions) and its endpoints (the API surface). The contract is SPEC-taf-v1-001-01. Why it exists A test suite is usually a pile of files that encode knowledge of the SUT invisibly. Here the knowledge is written down once, as data: the same model generates the Python suite and the Java suite, refuses broken input with named reasons, and later is the graph that model-based walks run on. Change the model, regenerate, and the suite's diff is the change — visible in a pull request. In this repo sut.model.json per SUT (none yet — lane A-1 builds the loader). Read SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 for the clauses and the nine refusals."},{"t":"The locator ladder","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-locator-ladder","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-003-01-the-locator-ladder.html","s":"draft","x":"The locator ladder Definition The ordered preference for how a test finds a UI element: 1. role + name — what the element is to a user (button \"Save\") 2. label / text / placeholder — what it says 3. testid (data-testid) — a deliberate marker the SUT provides 4. css / xpath — how it happens to be built; last resort, flagged fragile Why it exists Tests that find elements by structure break when structure changes; tests that find them by meaning break only when meaning changes. The ladder is Playwright's own guidance made mechanical: in sut.model.json a locator declares one of the rungs and :bad-locator refuses anything else, so the preference is enforced by the model, not by habit. In this repo SPEC-taf-v1-001-01, elements clause. The emitters translate each rung to the platform's locator API (getbyrole → getByRole)."},{"t":"Arrange–Act–Assert","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-arrange-act-assert","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-arrange-act-assert.html","s":"draft","x":"Arrange–Act–Assert Definition The shape of a readable test: arrange the preconditions, act once, assert the outcome. Three phases, in that order, visible in the code. python def testticketmovestotriage(boardpage): ticket = boardpage.newticket(\"the report\") arrange ticket.moveto(\"triage\") act expect(boardpage.ticket(\"the report\")).tobein(\"triage\") assert Why it exists A test is documentation that runs. When the three phases are separated, a reader sees what is tested before how; when they interleave, the intent drowns in mechanics. In tafbase the phases come from the model: a journey's setup is its start state, the act is its steps, and the assertions are the state markers and expect-final the emitter writes for every arrival — so generated tests are AAA by construction. In this repo Generated suites under tools/ (lane B-2). Emitted tests carry @concept CONCEPT-taf-v1-004-01-arrange-act-assert."},{"t":"The pytest fixture","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-pytest-fixture","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-005-01-the-pytest-fixture.html","s":"draft","x":"The pytest fixture Definition A function pytest calls to provide something a test needs — a browser, a page, a base URL — and to clean it up after. yield splits the two halves: everything before it is setup, everything after is teardown that runs even when the test fails. python @pytest.fixture def page(browser): pg = browser.newpage() setup yield pg handed to the test pg.close() teardown, always scope= says how often the fixture is made: function (every test — the default), class, module, session (once for the whole run). Why it exists Tests should not repeat their plumbing. Fixtures give each test a fresh, named dependency — and the scope gives control over cost: a browser is expensive (session), a clean page is cheap (function). In this repo generated//conftest.py — the emitted suites get baseurl, browser and page fixtures for free."},{"t":"The browser context","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-browser-context","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-006-01-the-browser-context.html","s":"draft","x":"The browser context Definition Playwright's unit of isolation: a browser.newcontext() is an independent session — own cookies, storage, cache — inside one browser. Each test gets a fresh context, so one test's login or localStorage can never leak into the next. Why it exists The honest alternative is a fresh browser per test, which is slow, or a shared browser, which leaks state and makes failures order-dependent. A context is fast and isolated — test independence by construction, which is what makes generated suites safe to run in any order or in parallel later. In this repo generated//conftest.py — the page fixture creates and closes a context around every test."},{"t":"Auto-waiting assertions","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01-auto-waiting","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-007-01-auto-waiting.html","s":"draft","x":"Auto-waiting assertions Definition expect(locator).tobevisible() does not check once — it retries until the page catches up or the timeout fails the test. Playwright's locators and assertions wait for the condition instead of racing the browser. Why it exists The classic cause of flaky tests is the race between the test and the app: click() before the button renders, assert before the page settles. Explicit sleep() calls are how suites rot. Auto-waiting makes the honest default also the fast one — the test proceeds the moment the SUT is actually ready. In this repo generated//pages.py — expectarrived() is an auto-waiting marker check; every state transition in a journey waits on the SUT's proof, never on a timer."},{"t":"The page object","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-008-01-the-page-object","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-008-01-the-page-object.html","s":"draft","x":"The page object Definition A class that is a page or state of the SUT: it holds the page's locators and its meaningful operations, so tests say board.expectarrived() instead of repeating selectors. python class BoardPage: def init(self, page): self.marker = page.locator(\".ikc-dock-theme\") def expectarrived(self): expect(self.marker).tobevisible() Why it exists Locators are the most-fragile line in a suite. When they live in one class per state, a UI change is a one-place fix — and tests read as intent (\"arrive at the board\"), not mechanics. In tafbase the pattern is stricter: a page object's marker is the proof of arrival the model declares, so the class also knows when its page is real. In this repo generated//pages.py — one class per declared state, generated from states in the model (SPEC-taf-v1-001-01)."},{"t":"The JUnit lifecycle","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-009-01-the-junit-lifecycle","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-009-01-the-junit-lifecycle.html","s":"draft","x":"The JUnit lifecycle Definition JUnit 5's fixture mechanism: annotated methods JUnit runs around tests. @BeforeAll/@AfterAll run once per class (static, expensive setup like launching a browser); @BeforeEach/@AfterEach bracket every @Test (fresh context, fresh page). It is the same idea as the pytest fixture — setup and guaranteed teardown — expressed as annotations on a base class. java @BeforeAll static void launch() { browser = playwright.chromium().launch(); } @BeforeEach void fresh() { context = browser.newContext(); page = context.newPage(); } @AfterEach void closeContext() { context.close(); } @AfterAll static void shutdown() { browser.close(); } Why it exists Generated Java tests get the same isolation contract as the pytest ones (session browser, per-test context) through a different mechanism — proof that the model's semantics survive the language change and only the idiom moves. In this repo generated-java//src/test/java/taf/generated/BaseTest.java — every generated JUnit suite inherits its lifecycle from this class."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone","i":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone Decided: 2026-10-14 · George Petrov + Devin Decisions 1. tafbase is its own repository and its own corpus. It does not live inside IKC3. gaks.project.json declares it; the static GAKS bundle of PLAN-ikc-v3-079-01 delivers the corpus tools. This is the first real consumer of the foreign-project path. 2. The DSL is JSON, not EDN. The SUT model must be readable and writable from Java and Python with no Clojure dependency — the same reason gaks.project.json replaced the EDN declaration in IKC3. 3. Generate, don't interpret. Emitters write committed, reviewable test files (testlogin.py, LoginJourneyTest.java). A generic runtime interpreter was considered and refused: generated code diffs in a PR are legible; hidden magic is not. 4. Tracker integrations are declared adapters, not a hard dependency. tafbase core runs with no IKC3. The IKC3 adapter (fault reports, repaired-on-merge) is the first sink and the demo loop; JIRA and GitHub Issues adapters are additions of the same kind. 5. The IKC3 tracker is the first SUT, not a component of tafbase. Dogfooding means tests exercise the real platform and file reports into it — not that tafbase embeds it. 6. MIT license. The goals are exposure and adoption; copyleft and dual-licensing are refused. Consequences Doc ids take the taf subproject prefix (PLAN-taf-v1-NNN-NN); numbering is this repository's own. The IKC3 deployment tracker can file tickets for tafbase work only if this corpus is registered as a vault of the deployment — an open question, deferred to the first lane."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01 — the delivered project is a learning surface","i":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01 — the delivered project is a learning surface Decided: 2026-10-14 · George Petrov + Devin Context The precedent is Python-Selenium-Chrome-Demo/v.0.1/docs/concepts.md: a hand-written glossary of 60 concepts — Python, Selenium, pytest, design patterns, testing — each with a definition, an example, and a \"Used in\" pointer. It was good teaching material and it could rot: the pointers were prose, checked by nobody. Decisions 7. The corpus teaches. CONCEPT documents in arch-root/concepts/ cover the implicit and explicit concepts the project embodies, across four domains: the language and runner (Python: fixtures, yield, dataclasses; pytest/JUnit: discovery, markers, parametrize), the tool (Playwright/Selenium: locators, auto-waiting, browser contexts), test design (AAA, page object, isolation, flaky tests), and the DSL itself (model, emitter, manifest, parity, refusal). A concept is a page — a definition, a small example, where it lives in this repo — never a reference manual. 8. Citations are checked, not promised. Generated code carries @concept CONCEPT-taf-v1-NNN-NN- comment lines, the form the GAKS code.annotation-pattern already checks (code-link-unresolved fails when a cited concept has no document). The emitter decides which concepts each generated file demonstrates and writes the annotations; the manifest records the concept ids per test. An annotation with no document is a defect the checker refuses. A document nothing cites is not: a concept may be written before the code that embodies it — the learning path marks such pages as waiting on their lane, and a corpus report may list them, but the check does not fail on them. 9. A guided path orders the glossary. The corpus is flat and wiki-linked, but a learner needs a sequence. One concept document — CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path — is the ordered index: what a test is → AAA → locators → fixtures → the model → the emitters. Junior-first; each entry names its prerequisites. 10. PRINCIPLE documents join the corpus. tafbase's gaks.project.json declares the PRINCIPLE kind so the platform's own rules (DSL First, release-note-first, one-form-only) are corpus documents that code and plans can cite, not folklore in a README. Consequences Every emitter lane ships the concept documents its generated code cites; the corpus check is the gate — a lane that emits an uncited concept does not pass. The learning surface is a property of the delivered bundle, not of ikc3: gaks/site/ renders the concepts and their backlinks with no server, on tafbase.com or off a disk."},{"t":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework","i":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase","h":"doc/plans/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework Status: in progress tafbase declares a system under test (SUT) once, as data, and generates real test suites from the declaration. Python+Playwright is the first emitter; Java is the second, and the second exists to make the parity claim true. Generated suites report into pluggable tracker adapters; the first adapter is IKC3's fault reports, so a red test files a bug into the very platform the tests exercise. Release note (draft) New: a SUT declared once in JSON runs as a Python+Playwright suite; the same declaration refuses the same broken model the same way. Accepted by: T-taf-001 (parity of refusal, env -i, python3 only) New: a failed generated test files a fault report into a configured tracker through a declared adapter; the IKC3 adapter is the first. Accepted by: T-taf-002 Internal: the Java+Playwright emitter proves the same declaration generates equivalent suites in two languages. Accepted by: T-taf-003 (Python and Java emitters agree on the suite) What this does not do v1 generates static journeys only. Model-based generation — walks of the SUT graph, coverage-directed paths, journal replay — is declared in ADR-taf-v1-001-01 and arrives by lanes, not all at once. Lanes ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1 — the SUT model is JSON The contract: SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model. A SUT declares states, transitions and journeys in one JSON document. The loader validates shape, refusals name the broken clause, and no ambiguous form is guessed — one form only. Release note (draft) New: sut.model.json declares pages, actions and journeys; a broken model names its fault. Accepted by: T-taf-001 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/B-2 — the Python+Playwright emitter From a valid model, taf emit --lang python writes a runnable Playwright+pytest suite: page objects, journey tests, and a manifest mapping each test back to the model element that produced it. Release note (draft) New: one declaration produces a committed, reviewable pytest suite. Accepted by: T-taf-004 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/C-3 — tracker adapters and fault reports A test outcome flows to a declared sink. The IKC3 adapter files a fault report and links it to the test's model element; adapters are a declared kind so JIRA or GitHub Issues are additions, not rewrites. Release note (draft) New: a red test files a fault report into IKC3, named for its model element; the report repairs when the fix lands. Accepted by: T-taf-002 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/D-4 — the Java emitter proves parity --lang java emits the equivalent Playwright-Java + JUnit suite. Parity means: same model, same refusal text, same test names, same journey steps — the discipline measured in PLAN-ikc-v3-079-01 applied to a second axis. Release note (draft) New: the same declaration generates the Java suite; both emitters agree or both refuse. Accepted by: T-taf-003 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/E-5 — the tracker is the first SUT IKC3's tracker is the demo"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model.html","s":"draft","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone The design input of lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1. This document is the contract: a loader and an emitter are correct together when each refusal and each generated behavior below holds. Purpose A system under test (SUT) is declared once, as one JSON document. The declaration is the model. From it, emitters write runnable suites (Python+Playwright, later Java+Playwright) that follow Playwright's own rules: semantic locators, auto-waiting assertions, one journey per test. The same document is the graph the MBT lanes walk later — states and transitions are first-class from v1, so no model is written twice. The document One file per SUT, sut.model.json by convention. Top level: json { \"sut\": {\"name\": \"ikc3-tracker\", \"base-url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:4855\"}, \"states\": {}, \"elements\": {}, \"actions\": {}, \"journeys\": {}, \"endpoints\": {} } sut name (string, required) names the SUT in refusals and the manifest. base-url (string, required) is where journeys begin. No environment indirection in v1: a different deployment is a different file, or a later lane adds env. states A state is a place a journey can be — a page, a dialog, a verified condition. A state is recognized, not assumed: json \"states\": { \"board\": { \"route\": \"/index.html?island=board\", \"marker\": {\"role\": \"heading\", \"name\": \"Board\"} } } route (optional) is the path under base-url that reaches the state directly. marker (required) is a locator that a generated test asserts visible after every arrival — a state is entered only when the SUT proves it. That one rule is what makes generated tests self-verifying instead of hopeful. elements Named locators, declared once, reused everywhere: json \"elements\": { \"new-ticket-button\": {\"role\": \"button\", \"name\": \"New ticket\"}, \"title-field\": {\"label\": \"Title\"}, \"report-card\": {\"testid\": \"report-card\"}, \"legacy-panel\": {\"css\": \".panel--legacy\"} } THE LOCATOR LADDER is the \"proper use\" clause, encoded, not documented: 1. role + name — the accessibility query, preferred 2. label / text / placeholder — user-visible queries 3. testid — the deliberate escape (data-testid) 4. css / xpath — the last resort, allowed and flagged fragile in the manifest A locator with no usable form is refused :bad-locator. The ladder is the emitter's only ordering; a model does not say which rung a locator sits on, the shape does. actions An action is a step the SUT offers — the transitions of the graph: json \"actions\": { \"move-to-triage\": { \"from\": \"backlog\", \"to\": \"triage\", \"do\": [ {\"click\": \"new-ticket-button\"}, {\"fill\": {\"element\": \"title-field\", \"value\": \"the report\"}}, {\"click\": \"save-button\"}, {\"api\": \"move-ticket\", \"args\": {\"category\": \"triage\"}} ] } } from and to name states and are optional per action, required for the graph to be a graph: an action with both is an edge. do is an ordered list of steps: click / fill / check / select — on a named element navigate — to a "}];
